Richard Pearson, US Citizen, Recalls More Horrific Details from Torture

1. “Are you sure?”

Pearson has been tortured horrifically in the nation’s capital. His account — that of a gay African American PhD scholar — is nothing less than one of the worst in modern history. His assailants regularly inserted voices into his head, especially when he was in his apartment in downtown Washington, DC. Pearson maintains his intellectualism and analytical mind. After all, he is a researcher.

Pearson was speculating on a root cause behind his torture. “It’s got to be a brain disease! They don’t see me as human! They cannot understand the profound and severe consequences — morally, legally, constitutionally. Just as a human being. There must be some severance in the brain that prevents understanding.”

Pearson is in his apartment. They are listening — albeit illegally and in flagrant violation of the US Constitution. They have a response.

“Are you sure?”

Pearson heard it in my head. It was clear. In fact, he says that they repeated the same question that day and over subsequent days. For Pearson, it was yet another manifestation of the brain disease. “This brain disease is very severe. I need to underscore this emphatically. It creates a God-like complex of total control and power.” In fact, Pearson’s horrific account — from being pinned or forced to move to locations, inserted dreams (including pornography), voices, thoughts, even music, forced speech, forced keystrokes, computer and phone hacking, and the sheer scale of violence — strongly substantiated that he confronted individuals with something severe in their mental reasoning.

2. “Isn’t that cool? Would you pay for that?” (Montreal, Canada)

Pearson had arrived in Montreal near midnight during an asylum attempt in Canada. He was tortured at a black site at the US-Canadian border. He discussed it in detail here. Nevertheless, he was permitted to travel to Montreal where he had visited twice before. His prospect wasn’t exactly good. Canadian border officials had declared Pearson as unable to “understand the nature of the proceedings.” They had declared him mentally incompetent. He is not. Had he stayed in Canada, he would have likely been placed under an involuntary psychiatric hold.

Pearson had not yet decided to leave Montreal. He initially left the bus terminal to find the closest shelter but struggled with the exact directions since he had no phone. Pearson describes a disturbing incident. It should be noted that Pearson already knows that “pinning” — a word that he uses to describe mind control technology that can force people to move to geographic locations — exists. It had happened on numerous occasions in Washington, DC.

Pearson also saw a high concentration of people on the street in bright red and black — the same as in Washington, DC. “I am immediately triggered. It’s really late at night. At this point, it’s after midnight. Within 4–7 blocks after leaving the train station, it was like 6 or so people. It’s the same thing!” Pearson is not paranoid. He is vigilant. Those are two very different things. Pearson is watching his surroundings as he tries to make his third or so attempt to find the nearest shelter.

Pearson has a thought — that he should stand next to a man standing outside a convenience store, which French Canadians call a “depanneur”. Pearson did not suspect pinning. “This technology uses the mind against itself! I thought to myself, ‘Stand next to that man.’ I rationalized a reason, but it doesn’t really matter. In retrospect, it made no sense. Besides, I was on my way to seek shelter.” Pearson was right. It didn’t make any sense, but he did. Then, the man’s friend appears out of the store.

“This white man was in a bright red shirt and black pants — exactly the colors that had been stalking and harassing me in Washington, DC and just moments earlier on the streets of Montreal.”

