3. The Toothbrush (August 12, 2026)

In 2026, Pearson began going to a forest near Georgetown. “It was to get away from the violence. It became my sanctuary.” Eventually, the torture compromised that space too. He has discussed it — voice insertion, including someone using technology to tell him that he is a “special agent”. Pearson is not a special agent. He is enslaved — a prisoner in an invisible prision who has a chip in his skill. “It’s modern slavery. Plain and simple. The US brought back slavery, and it strongly appears that there is a privatized component to it — hence the violence and technology use in Washington, DC and Montreal.”

Pearson has an idea to bathe in the forest. His hygiene is not great, but he is a man being tortured in the shadow of the US Capitol. He has soap and a washcloth on him. It is unclear if the forest bathing was his idea or something else, but he does. He sits and lathers on the soap, then he would use his water bottle to rinse. Right there in front of him, a toothbrush sticking out of the ground.

“Is this coincidental or a demonstration of a power obsession? I find it entirely consistent that this is the psychosis that I am confronting — a God-like complex that can move me to a location at a toothbrush.” The message for Pearson is to brush his teeth. Pearson did not place the object. He is a researcher documenting his own account of torture in Washington, DC.

Pearson adds, “Use of these technologies are changing the neurological structure of their brains. It’s not just the god-like complex, but the assumption that they are going to have complete and total immunity. That only fuels more.”

“And let me add this. I am not the only victim. In fact, I think it’s systematized.”
